[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce any outage on PC11 or its switch port. From PC11 (172.16.20.11), L2 and L3 connectivity are normal:
- Same-VLAN neighbors reachable: successful pings to 172.16.20.10 (PC10) and 172.16.20.9 (PC9) with 0% loss and sub‑ms to low‑ms RTTs.
- Off‑subnet routing works: PC11 can reach 172.16.10.5 (VLAN 10) and 10.5.0.1 (VLAN 5), proving default routing is functional.
- Local config is sane: eth0 is UP with 172.16.20.11/24 and default via 172.16.20.254; ARP shows neighbors and the gateway MAC learned (00:01:97:e4:04:02).

One thing that can mislead: direct pings to the gateway IP (172.16.20.254) did not answer during testing, yet traffic is clearly being forwarded through it (TTL decrements and cross‑VLAN pings succeed). This is consistent with ICMP echo disabled/rate‑limited on the VLAN 20 gateway interface, not a switching or port issue.

Mechanism: The user likely validated only by pinging the gateway, which does not reply to echo requests, giving the appearance of a total outage. Actual data forwarding and neighbor reachability are intact.

Localization: VLAN 20 access on switch2 (pc11 on access_vlan20_port6) is healthy; routing via the 172.16.20.254 gateway is operating.

Root cause: No fault found; the report likely stems from testing against a gateway SVI that does not respond to ICMP echo.

Corrective fix: None required. If desired, enable ICMP echo on the 172.16.20.254 interface or instruct helpdesk to validate with a routed destination (e.g., 172.16.10.5) instead of the gateway IP. Do not move the port. 
=== END ===